{"question": "A 13-month-old boy is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. Physical examination shows hepatosplenomegaly. A venous blood sample obtained for routine screening tests is milky. After refrigeration, a creamy supernatant layer appears on top of the sample. Genetic analysis shows a mutation in the apolipoprotein C-II gene (APOC2) on chromosome 19. This patient is at greatest risk for developing which of the following complications?", "options": {"A": "Acute pancreatitis", "B": "Myocardial infarction", "C": "Corneal arci", "D": "Cholesterol embolization syndrome", "E": "Cerebrovascular accident"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Gastroenterology", "Hematology", "Genetics", "Metabolic Disorders"], "option_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Genetics"], "question_analyses": {"Pediatrics": "The medical scenario describes a 13-month-old boy with hepatosplenomegaly and a milky appearance of his venous blood sample, which upon refrigeration, shows a creamy supernatant layer. Genetic analysis reveals a mutation in the apolipoprotein C-II gene (APOC2) on chromosome 19. This presentation is characteristic of LPL (Lipoprotein Lipase) deficiency, a rare genetic disorder that affects the breakdown of triglycerides in lipoproteins. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n1. **Hepatosplenomegaly**: This is a common finding in LPL deficiency, often due to the accumulation of triglycerides in the liver and spleen. 2. **Milky Appearance of Blood**: This is known as chylous blood, which is a result of the accumulation of chylomicrons (a type of lipoprotein) in the blood. The milky appearance is due to the presence of large amounts of triglycerides. 3. **Creamy Supernatant Layer**: This is a hallmark of chylous blood. When the blood sample is refrigerated, the triglycerides coalesce and form a creamy layer on top of the sample. 4. **APOC2 Gene Mutation**: Apolipoprotein C-II (APOC2) is a cofactor for lipoprotein lipase (LPL).."